# Keep the Newt tunnel up, and preserve self-updates across reboots.
#
# --- watchdog ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Newt's registration handshake with the Pangolin server fails intermittently:
# it authenticates, logs "SendMessageInterval timed out ... newt/wg/register",
# and then keeps the process alive forever without ever retrying. Measured on a
# live server, roughly a quarter of starts land in that state, and none of them
# recover on their own - so without this a site can sit silently disconnected
# after a boot or a network blip until somebody notices and presses Connect.
#
# The check is cheap (a pgrep and a stat of the health file) and does nothing
# at all unless the service is meant to be connected but is not: pressing
# Disconnect removes the intent marker, so a deliberate disconnect is never
# undone. Five minutes keeps a failed reconnect short without hammering the
# server if it is genuinely down.
*/5 * * * * /etc/rc.d/rc.newt watchdog >/dev/null 2>&1

# --- self-update sync -------------------------------------------------------
# Only does anything when "Allow Newt to update itself" is set to Yes in
# Settings > Pangolin Newt. Newt replaces its own binary in /usr/local/bin,
# which lives in RAM on Unraid and is rebuilt on every boot, so without this
# the server would silently roll back to the plugin's bundled version at the
# next reboot. In steady state this is a single cmp of two files, and it is a
# no-op entirely when self-update is disabled (the default).
#
# Ten minutes is deliberately unhurried: Newt only checks for updates a couple
# of minutes after start and then every six hours, so there is nothing to catch
# more often than that, and the window for losing an update is a reboot within
# ten minutes of one landing.
*/10 * * * * /etc/rc.d/rc.newt sync-binary >/dev/null 2>&1
